<h2 id="nyc-edge">What makes side jobs in New York different</h2>
<p>Three things shape how you should approach side gigs in New York:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You don't need a car.</strong> Most of the high-demand work here runs on foot, bike or transit. That cuts your startup cost and your biggest ongoing expense.</li>
  <li><strong>People pay for convenience, and they pay well.</strong> Small apartments, long hours and walk-up buildings create demand for laundry pickup, furniture assembly, moving help, pet care and childcare that suburban households often handle themselves.</li>
  <li><strong>Regulation is real.</strong> Rideshare needs a TLC license, short-term rentals are tightly restricted, and vending needs permits. Pick a hustle that doesn't need a license, or budget the time to get one.</li>
</ul>
<p>The city's calendar also matters. The UN General Assembly in September, the US Open in Flushing in late summer, the NYC Marathon in early November, Thanksgiving through New Year's, and Fashion Weeks in February and September all spike demand for drivers, event staff, hospitality help and photographers.</p>

