<h2 id="street-level">Street-level gigs: cash this week</h2>
<p>These are the first eight picks because they pay fastest, need almost no money to start and fit New York's car-free reality.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="delivery">1. App-based food and grocery delivery</h3>
    <p>New York is one of the few US cities with a legal pay floor for app delivery. The Department of Consumer and Worker Protection sets the minimum pay rate for apps like DoorDash, Uber Eats, Grubhub and Instacart, and since April 1, 2026 it has been $22.13 an hour before tips for restaurant and grocery delivery. Midtown lunch, the Financial District and dense residential pockets like the Upper East Side, Astoria and Williamsburg keep orders close together. Rain and snow are paydays.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $22.13/hr minimum on deliveries, plus tips</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Bike, e-bike or transit</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Lunch and dinner rushes</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="dog-walking">2. Dog walking and pet sitting</h3>
    <p>Apartment dogs need midday walks, and owners working long hours in Midtown will pay for them. The Upper West Side, Park Slope, Brooklyn Heights, Long Island City and Hoboken-adjacent pockets are dense with dogs. Rover and Wag help you start; a tight route of regulars in a few buildings is where the money is. Holiday weeks book out early.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$35/walk, $60–$100/night</li><li><b>Startup:</b> A sturdy [[amazon:dog-leash|leash]] and treats</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Daytime availability</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="babysitting">3. Babysitting and after-school care</h3>
    <p>Date-night sitters, school pickups and backup care are in steady demand across Manhattan and brownstone Brooklyn. Care.com and Sittercity help, but parent group chats and building referrals fill calendars faster. CPR certification and a second language raise your rate.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$35/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$100 (CPR class)</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Evenings and weekends</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="moving-help">4. Moving help and furniture assembly</h3>
    <p>Fourth-floor walk-ups, narrow stairwells and the end-of-month lease cycle make strong backs valuable, especially around September 1 when students and new grads flood the city. TaskRabbit is busy with flat-pack assembly, TV mounting and moving labor. Team up with someone who owns a van to take bigger jobs.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $35–$70/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Drill and basic tools</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Weekends, month-end</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="cleaning">5. Apartment cleaning</h3>
    <p>Small apartments clean fast, which means more jobs per day. Recurring clients in Manhattan and Brooklyn want reliability more than anything. Move-out cleans around lease turnover pay a premium because tenants want their security deposit back.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $30–$50/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $50–$100 supplies</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Daytime schedules</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="event-staffing">6. Event staffing and brand ambassador work</h3>
    <p>Staffing agencies hire for galas, product launches, trade shows at the Javits Center, sampling campaigns and concerts at Madison Square Garden and Barclays Center. Fashion Week, the holiday party season and the US Open are peak periods. Bartending certifications open the best-paid shifts.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$35/hr, more for bartenders</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Outgoing people</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="errands">7. Errands, laundry and grocery runs</h3>
    <p>Wash-and-fold pickup, returns, pharmacy runs and waiting in line for a building repair visit are all things busy New Yorkers outsource. TaskRabbit and local Facebook groups work, and older residents in Queens and the Bronx often prefer a trusted neighbor over an app.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$35/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible days</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="background-acting">8. Background acting and focus groups</h3>
    <p>New York is a major film and TV production hub, and non-union background work is a legitimate way to earn a day rate on set. Paid market research and focus groups in Midtown also pay well for an hour or two. Use reputable casting agencies and never pay to be considered.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically a day rate; focus groups $75–$200 a session</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible schedules</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> If you deliver on an e-bike, buy one with a UL-certified battery. NYC law bans the sale of non-certified e-bikes and batteries, and lithium-ion battery fires are a serious risk in apartments. Never charge overnight unattended.</div>
