<h2 id="rideshare-tlc">Rideshare in NYC: the TLC route</h2>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">9. Rideshare driving with a TLC license</h3>
    <p>Driving for Uber or Lyft in the five boroughs is not a casual side gig. You need a TLC driver license (chauffeur-class license, medical exam, drug test, fingerprints, required courses and an exam) and a TLC-licensed vehicle. Because new for-hire vehicle licenses have been largely paused for years, most new drivers rent or lease a plated car, which turns this into something closer to a part-time job. Trips from JFK and LaGuardia, late nights in Manhattan and big-event exits are the best windows. Our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft and Uber guide</a> covers the platform side.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$40/hr gross before car costs</li><li><b>Startup:</b> License fees, courses, rented TLC car</li><li><b>Best for:</b> People driving 20+ hours a week</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
